# Intro
One 29.7 Msun star at the centre of an isolated box (no periodic images) of cold (50 K),
zero-metallicity molecular gas with 8 dense Gaussian clumps (peak density 100 times the clear gas,
clumps 0.12 to 0.32 box sizes from the star). The ISRF transport is on (`ISRF_propagation: 1`) with
the H2 line absorption of the transport on (`ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`), so the Lyman-Werner (LW)
light of the star is absorbed by the H2 lines along its path.

At a limited light speed (`ISRF_packet_light_speed: fixed`) the light that has not yet
reached a gas particle waits in a reception slot of that particle. A slot is the part of the
particle's reception that holds the light from one direction. When part of the waiting light is
released, the released light receives the mean H2 shielding of the whole waiting pool. This example
runs the cloud twice from the same initial conditions, with a limited and with an unlimited light
speed (no light waits), and checks that each run lies inside the range of the shares of its
own neighbours.

The speed of the limited arm is `ISRF_packet_light_speed_fraction_of_c` (0.99 in `params.yml`).

# Configure
This example needs geajay; official Grackle refuses `ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1` at start-up.
The cooling solver is the geajay default (`GeajayCooling:solver` is not set in `params.yml`).
No extra build flag is needed.
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=geajay_3 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-geajay=path/to/geajay --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix
make -j
```
The packet transport needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`; `run.sh` fetches and checks it.

# Run
```
./run.sh
```
`run.sh` fetches the glass file and the tables, writes the initial conditions, runs the unlimited
arm (directory `control`), then the limited arm (directory `limited`), then the check. It deletes
`snap` and `restart` in the current directory first. Both arms write snapshots at the same times.
`level`, `gas_density`, `gas_mass`, `h2_ratio` (initial `nH2/nH`, default 3e-5) and `n_threads`
override the defaults. The run keeps the gas cold and the H2 nearly frozen: photoionization, winds
and supernovae are off, the metallicity is zero, and the time step is set by the light speed
(64 steps of 1.47e-10 internal units). Both arms are non-periodic, so no light of periodic images
of the star reaches the gas.

# Checking
`python3 checkH2WaitingLight.py --limited limited --control control`

A particle releases a weighted mean of the light that waits for it, so its H2
transmission and column must lie between the smallest and the largest value that its
neighbours closer to the star hand to it (its shares). The check reads the particles
between 2 smoothing lengths and half a box minus 2 smoothing lengths from the star whose
H2 density is nearly constant, at every snapshot that both runs hold, and tests that:
- the limited run lies inside the range of its own shares;
- the control run lies inside the range of its own shares;
- the two runs differ by at most the width of the range of the shares of both runs (this follows
  from the two tests above and is not a separate test).

The check prints the first and last snapshot it read and why the comparison ends. On one
core it takes about 45 minutes for the default resolution (41 snapshots per run). It exits
with a non-zero status when it fails.
